{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019PeTq9qR7ueEtryZssNuW4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LTL4KeZhVbDxcACWZMPA29","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment — R1: Methods & Experiments\n\n**Venue:** ICLR 2026 (rating scale: {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10})\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 8: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 4: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 0: Trivial or wrong\n\n**Your focus:** Methodological soundness, experimental rigor, statistical validity, reproducibility.\n\n**Paper summary:** \"Psychometric Characterization of Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale DIF Analysis of MMLU.\" Applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items). 4 claims: (1) 31.3% MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF; (2) web-overlap contamination labels are mismatched for item-level validation; (3) semi-synthetic ∆TPR framework with cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation; (4) DIF items serve as diagnostic lens, not quality filter (ρ≈0.997 after removal).\n\n**Key data to evaluate:**\n\n### Claim 1 — Temporal DIF (§4.1)\n- Table 1: 31.3% B+C flagged items, random-split sanity 0.18%\n- Table 2: Cohort sensitivity — temporal 31.3%, ability 2.9%, within-family 61.3%\n- Table 3: Architecture stratification — 99.98% decoder-only, Jaccard=1.0 after removing 1 non-decoder model\n- IRT fit: RMSEA=0.089, CFI=0.72 — poor 1D IRT fit acknowledged\n- Domain decomposition: accuracy improvement explains ≤7.6% of C%\n\n### Claim 2 — Ground Truth Meta-Critique (§4.2)\n- Enrichment ratio 0.96≈1.0 against Li et al. labels\n- Argument: web-overlap = global property; DIF = differential property — construct mismatch\n- Brief section, largely conceptual\n\n### Claim 3 — Semi-Synthetic Framework (§4.3)\n- Table 4: Dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0, ∆TPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, ∆FPR=0.000\n- Table 5: Cross-subject vs within-subject matching — FPR inflation +0.186 eliminated\n- Table 6: GSM8K replication (1,319 items × 6,702 models), ∆TPR=+0.818\n\n### Claim 4 — DIF as Diagnostic Lens (§4.4)\n- ρ≈0.997 after removing 31.3% DIF items\n- Table 7: z-score comparison — DIF z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157\n- Table 8: GSM1K correlation r=-0.478, N=33, CI wide, Spearman upper bound -0.031\n\n### Supplementary Analyses (§5)\n- §5.1 RL robustness: N=13 reasoning-RL, TOST inconclusive (p=0.14)\n- §5.2 ConStat: Jaccard=0.08 with DIF, 15 models\n- §5.3 DVD: Jaccard=0.05, primarily verbatim detection\n- §5.4 Ensemble: AUROC=0.62, weight stability=0.43, circularity with Claim 2\n- §5.5 Bifactor IRT: CFI=0.81, TLI=0.78, general factor 34%, equating ρ=0.94\n- §5.6 Item generation: ρ=0.58 vs target 0.75 — negative result\n- §5.7 Cumulative Bayesian DIF: simulation-only, power 0.41→0.74 over 4 admins\n- §5.8 Graceful degradation: validity floor ~35%\n- §5.9 Item-Focused Trees: 78% concordance with MH, Jaccard=0.68\n\n### Key methodological questions to address:\n1. Is the 1D IRT misspecification a fatal flaw or adequately addressed? (CFI=0.72, 98% models |Lz|>2, bifactor pilot shows C% drops only to ~28-29%)\n2. Is the semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect→correct flip) a valid contamination model?\n3. Are the statistical tests properly applied? (MH χ², ETS dual criteria, permutation tests, BCa bootstrap)\n4. Is N=33 for GSM1K correlation appropriately caveated?\n5. How well does the paper handle multiple comparisons across 12,508 items?\n6. Is cross-subject matching a genuine methodological contribution or an obvious choice?\n\n### Experiment coverage assessment:\nThe review_context lists these imagined experiments (check coverage):\n- plan_001 (MMLU DIF): ✓ fully covered in §4.1\n- plan_002 (RL robustness): ✓ covered in §5.1, appropriately caveated\n- plan_003a (ConStat): ✓ covered in §5.2\n- plan_003b (DVD): ✓ covered in §5.3\n- plan_003c (Ensemble): ✓ covered in §5.4, reported as negative\n- plan_004 (GSM8K): ✓ partially covered (Steps 1-2 in §4.3.2, Step 3 in §4.4.3)\n- plan_005 (Bifactor IRT): ✓ covered in §5.5\n- plan_006 (Item generation): ✓ covered as negative result in §5.6\n- plan_007 (Bayesian DIF): ✓ covered in §5.7\n- plan_008 (Graceful degradation): ✓ covered in §5.8\n- plan_009 (IFT): ✓ covered in §5.9\n- plan_010 (Architecture stratification): ✓ covered in §4.1.3\n- plan_011 (Semi-synthetic): ✓ covered in §4.3\n\n**Deliverable:** A structured review with:\n1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Strengths (bulleted, with specific table/figure references)\n3. Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Major/Minor)\n4. Questions for authors\n5. Preliminary scores: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (0/2/4/6/8/10), Confidence (1-5)","reviewer_id":"1","round_number":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":671,"cache_read_input_tokens":67237,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":671},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17abd94c-441a-4590-97d9-9458f106f7c0","uuid":"7f96df1b-61a8-4b65-92b2-3891ad15ce45"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LTL4KeZhVbDxcACWZMPA29","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_f025582a920d3f79beb59dfc17929017:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17abd94c-441a-4590-97d9-9458f106f7c0","uuid":"98aa5453-126e-47c7-b0ac-f8320ff7c8f0","timestamp":"2026-05-21T07:02:03.578Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_f025582a920d3f79beb59dfc17929017:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_f025582a920d3f79beb59dfc17929017:reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019PeTq9qR7ueEtryZssNuW4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XZNRGBU4neMM1vcddPuxwg","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment — R2: Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Venue:** ICLR 2026 (rating scale: {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10})\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 8: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 4: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 0: Trivial or wrong\n\n**Your focus:** Novelty, significance, positioning against related work, clarity, impact.\n\n**Paper summary:** \"Psychometric Characterization of Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale DIF Analysis of MMLU.\" Applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items). 4 claims: (1) 31.3% MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF; (2) web-overlap contamination labels are mismatched for item-level validation; (3) semi-synthetic ∆TPR framework with cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation; (4) DIF items serve as diagnostic lens, not quality filter (ρ≈0.997 after removal).\n\n**Key novelty questions to evaluate:**\n\n### 1. Is \"applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks\" sufficiently novel?\n- The paper explicitly states \"our contribution is empirical, not algorithmic\" (§2)\n- MH-DIF is a 60+ year old method; ETS classification is standard\n- The novelty claim rests on: (a) first large-scale application to LLM evaluation, (b) the empirical findings themselves, (c) the semi-synthetic ∆TPR framework, (d) the cross-subject matching innovation\n- Risk: reviewers may say \"this is just MH-DIF at scale\"\n\n### 2. Related work positioning\n- §2 covers: contamination detection (Min-K%, ConStat, n-gram), DIF literature (Mantel-Haenszel, Holland-Thayer, ETS, Item-Focused Trees), LLM benchmark methodology (Growing Pains, METABENCH)\n- **Missing references to check:** Does the paper cite/discuss:\n  - Benchmark saturation / ceiling effects literature?\n  - Other psychometric approaches to LLM evaluation (IRT-based scoring, Rasch models)?\n  - Data contamination surveys (e.g., Li et al. 2024 survey)?\n  - HELM, BIG-bench, and other large-scale evaluation efforts?\n  - Test security literature from educational measurement?\n  - Measurement invariance / factorial invariance literature beyond DIF?\n  - Recent work on dynamic benchmarking (LiveBench, Chatbot Arena)?\n\n### 3. Significance of findings\n- \"31.3% items are unstable\" — is this surprising or expected?\n- \"ρ≈0.997 after removal\" — does the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing add to knowledge?\n- Ground truth meta-critique — is the web-overlap criticism novel? Others have raised this.\n- Cross-subject matching — is this a real contribution or an obvious methodological choice?\n\n### 4. Clarity and presentation\n- 10-page paper with 18 tables — is this too dense?\n- §5 (Supplementary Analyses) takes 3 pages for 9 sub-analyses — appropriate depth?\n- \"Instability ≠ Invalidity\" framing — effective or diluting?\n- Abstract: 4 findings packed into one abstract — clear or overwhelming?\n- Writing quality: formal, clear, but dense\n\n### 5. Impact assessment\n- Who would use this? Benchmark creators? Model evaluators? Contamination researchers?\n- Does the paper change how the community thinks about benchmark evaluation?\n- Is the ∆TPR framework likely to be adopted?\n- Does the \"diagnostic lens\" framing lead to actionable insights?\n\n### 6. Comparison with concurrent/prior work\n- How does this compare to:\n  - Li et al.'s contamination survey (which provides the web-overlap labels being critiqued)?\n  - METABENCH paper itself (which this builds on)?\n  - ConStat and Min-K% papers (which are positioned as complementary)?\n  - Recent psychometric analyses in NLP (if any)?\n\n**Deliverable:** A structured review with:\n1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Strengths (bulleted, focusing on novelty and significance)\n3. Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Major/Minor, focusing on positioning and impact)\n4. Questions for authors\n5. Missing references (specific papers/areas)\n6. Preliminary scores: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (0/2/4/6/8/10), Confidence (1-5)","reviewer_id":"2","round_number":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":671,"cache_read_input_tokens":67237,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":671},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17abd94c-441a-4590-97d9-9458f106f7c0","uuid":"8e7687f5-6b1f-4909-8e9d-b84417a1f392"}
