{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y2k8m2NWy9MX9wpHrVZ34C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Cp9B4fALfSRTmByQ7ChLiN","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Psychometric Characterization of Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks\n\n**Paper:** \"Psychometric Characterization of Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale DIF Analysis of MMLU\"\n**Venue:** ICLR 2026 (rating scale: {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10})\n**Review mode:** Full roundtable (R1: Methods & Experiments; R2: Novelty & Related Work)\n**Date:** 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), reporting that 31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS B+C classification) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. Four claims are advanced: (1) temporal item instability at scale; (2) a ground truth meta-critique showing web-overlap labels are construct-mismatched for item-level contamination validation; (3) a semi-synthetic ∆TPR framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates FPR inflation; (4) DIF as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter (ρ ≈ 0.997 after removing flagged items). Nine supplementary analyses cover RL robustness, alternative detectors (ConStat, DVD), ensemble fusion, bifactor IRT, item generation, cumulative Bayesian DIF, graceful degradation, and Item-Focused Trees — most yielding negative or inconclusive results.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Reviewer Scores\n\n### R1 (Methods & Experiments)\n\n| Dimension | Score | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Soundness | 2/4 | Major unresolved issues: absent FDR correction on 12,508 tests (W2); 1D IRT failure with CFI=0.72 inadequately addressed by bifactor pilot (W1) |\n| Presentation | 3/4 | Tables well-structured; dose-response framing clear; limitation section exemplary |\n| Contribution | 3/4 | Cross-subject matching (S3) and large-scale DIF application are genuine contributions |\n| Overall | **4/10** | Borderline — strong conceptual framing and honest reporting, but two major statistical gaps require resolution before the 31.3% headline can stand |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | |\n\n### R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n| Dimension | Score | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Soundness | 2/4 | Methodology technically competent but poor IRT fit undermines measurement foundation; ρ≈0.997 contradicts severity framing |\n| Presentation | 2/4 | Nine supplementary analyses create structural incoherence; central practical implication is self-contradictory without reconciliation |\n| Contribution | 2/4 | Scale is novel; core method is not; \"diagnostic lens\" promise undelivered; no actionable practitioner guidance |\n| Overall | **4/10** | Below threshold — empirically competent but novelty case not made; related work substantially incomplete; central implication self-contradictory |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | |\n\n### Converged Scores\n\n| Dimension | Converged | Range |\n|-----------|-----------|-------|\n| Soundness | **2/4** | Both R1 and R2 independently arrived at 2/4 |\n| Presentation | **2.5/4** | R1=3, R2=2 |\n| Contribution | **2.5/4** | R1=3, R2=2 |\n| **Overall** | **4/10** | Both converge at 4 (marginally below acceptance threshold) |\n| Confidence | **4/5** | Both reviewers |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Strengths (Converged)\n\n**S1 [Both] — Unprecedented scale and rigorous sanity checks.** The 5,227-model × 12,508-item matrix is the largest psychometric DIF dataset assembled for LLM evaluation. The random-split sanity check (0.18% vs. 31.3%) conclusively rules out statistical artifacts. The temporal partitioning by release date is a principled design choice grounded in deployment history.\n\n**S2 [Both] — Cross-subject matching is the strongest methodological contribution.** The decision to use performance on *other* MMLU subjects as the matching variable is the paper's most original move. Table 5's direct comparison — within-subject ∆FPR = +0.186 vs. cross-subject ∆FPR = 0.000 — is clean, novel evidence that standard matching contaminates the conditioning variable. This contribution is reusable by the broader community.\n\n**S3 [Both] — Dose-response calibration (Table 4).** The monotone ∆TPR trajectory (p = 0.0 → 0.5, ∆TPR = 0 → +0.508) with simultaneous ∆FPR = 0.000 at all injection rates is a controlled, proportional sensitivity demonstration rarely seen in contamination-detection papers. GSM8K replication (∆TPR = +0.818) provides cross-benchmark evidence.\n\n**S4 [R2 primary] — Web-overlap construct-mismatch critique (Claim 2).** The distinction between global exposure (web-overlap labels) and differential contamination (DIF) is the paper's sharpest conceptual contribution. Enrichment ≈ 1.0 is presented as an expected consequence of construct mismatch, not DIF failure — a useful corrective for the field.\n\n**S5 [Both] — Honest negative results reporting.** Both reviewers commend the candid documentation of failures: item generation ρ = 0.58 vs. target 0.75, ensemble circularity, RL underpowering (N = 13), bifactor equating missing target. This transparency raises credibility of positive findings.\n\n**S6 [R1 primary] — Circularity ablation (Table 7).** The z-score comparison across removal strategies (DIF z = −9.5 vs. accuracy-gap z = −157 vs. high-variance z = −108) effectively resolves the potential tautology concern that DIF items are simply the most informative items. This is a well-designed ablation.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Weaknesses (Converged)\n\n### Major\n\n**W1 — 1D IRT misspecification is inadequately resolved. [Both R1 and R2, Major]**\nCFI = 0.72 and 98% of models with |L_z| > 2 indicate fundamental misfit of 1D IRT to MMLU's 57-subject structure. MH-DIF conditions on total score as an ability proxy, which assumes some degree of unidimensionality. The bifactor pilot (CFI = 0.81, TLI = 0.78, general factor = 34%) only partially addresses this — CFI remains below the 0.90 threshold. The paper claims DIF findings are \"qualitatively robust\" (C% drops to ~28–29%) but does not report full bifactor-corrected DIF reanalysis or demonstrate that MH-DIF matching on bifactor general-factor scores yields comparable results. If the latent space is genuinely multidimensional, score-based matching groups models that are similar overall but differ on specific dimensions driving item performance — a form of residual confounding that could inflate or deflate the 31.3% headline.\n\n**W2 — Multiple comparisons across 12,508 items: FDR/FWER control absent. [R1, Major]**\nThe dual criterion (effect size |∆_MH| ≥ 1.5 + MH χ² p < 0.05) is applied to 12,508 simultaneous tests. At p < 0.05 with no correction, ~625 items would pass the χ² criterion by chance alone. The random-split sanity check (0.18%) provides empirical evidence that the combined dual criterion suppresses false positives, but this is one empirical demonstration, not a formal correction. The paper does not report what proportion of the 31.3% survives Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at q = 0.05 or q = 0.20. If the B+C rate drops substantially under FDR control, the headline claim requires revision.\n\n**W3 — Novelty threshold for ICLR is borderline. [R2, Major]**\nThe paper explicitly disclaims algorithmic novelty: \"our contribution is empirical, not algorithmic\" (§2). MH-DIF dates to 1959; ETS classification to 1988–1993. Applying well-understood procedures from psychometrics to LLM evaluation is closer to an empirical study than an ICLR main-conference contribution. The burden is on demonstrating that findings are surprising, consequential, or actionable — that case is weakened by ρ ≈ 0.997 showing rankings barely change. Cross-subject matching (S2) partially addresses the novelty concern but is a single methodological refinement, not a full framework.\n\n**W4 — Related work is substantially incomplete (only 10 references). [R2, Major]**\nFor a paper bridging psychometrics and ML evaluation, the reference list is extraordinarily sparse. Missing entire literatures:\n- **Dynamic/live benchmarking:** LiveBench, Chatbot Arena, WildBench — the field's primary operational response to benchmark contamination. If contamination can be avoided through item rotation, the paper must engage with why post-hoc DIF analysis of a static benchmark remains valuable.\n- **IRT for NLP:** Lalor et al. (2016, 2019), Vania et al. (2021), Rodriguez et al. (2021) — existing bridge work between psychometrics and NLP evaluation.\n- **Contamination detection:** Golchin & Surdeanu (2023), Deng et al. (2024) — directly relevant.\n- **Distribution shift:** Recht et al. (2019) on ImageNet temporal replication — foundational framing.\n\n### Moderate\n\n**W5 — \"Diagnostic lens\" claim is asserted but not demonstrated. [R2, Moderate]**\nClaim 4 states DIF provides a diagnostic lens for understanding cross-generational capability shifts, but the paper never characterizes *what* makes DIF-C items distinctive beyond domain-level correlation (ρ = 0.307). The ρ ≈ 0.997 finding simultaneously claims contamination exists at scale AND rankings are robust — these positions require explicit reconciliation. What evaluation validity failure does temporal DIF actually cause if rankings are preserved? What should a benchmark maintainer *do* with these findings? Actionable guidance is absent.\n\n**W6 — Injection model is simplistic and asymmetric. [R1, Moderate]**\nThe incorrect → correct flip is the most favorable contamination model for DIF detection. Real contamination includes partial exposure, chain-of-thought priming, and correct → correct-with-higher-confidence patterns. The ∆TPR = +0.508 at p = 0.5 essentially validates that a score-inflation detector detects score inflation — a tautological operating point. Detection power at low exploitation rates (p = 0.1, the practically relevant regime) is not explicitly reported. The claim that \"more complex patterns would produce larger DIF effects\" is plausible but unvalidated.\n\n**W7 — Nine supplementary analyses dilute focus. [R2, Moderate]**\n§5 contains nine sub-analyses in 3 pages, most with negative or inconclusive results (item generation failure, ensemble circularity, RL underpowering, cumulative Bayesian simulation-only, DVD low detection power). The cumulative effect is a paper that reads as a collection of pilot studies rather than a focused empirical contribution. A tighter paper with 4 claims + 2–3 focused supplementary analyses would be substantially stronger.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W8 — GSM1K correlation (Table 8) is underpowered. [Both, Minor]**\nN = 33 with Spearman CI [−0.687, −0.031] approaching zero. The minimum detectable effect at 80% power for N = 33 is approximately r = 0.47 — the observed r = −0.478 is essentially at the detection floor. This is exploratory at best, not confirmatory validation. The paper appropriately caveats but still positions it as supporting Claim 4.\n\n**W9 — ρ ≈ 0.997 understates potential practical impact at the top of the leaderboard. [R1, Minor]**\nThe global Spearman ρ applies to the full ranking of 5,227 models. At the top-10 or top-50 level — where deployment decisions are made — small absolute rank changes can have large practical consequences. Kendall's τ or NDCG@k for k ∈ {10, 50, 100} should be reported.\n\n**W10 — Architecture stratification (Table 3) is trivially informative. [R1, Minor]**\n99.98% decoder-only (5,226/5,227) makes removing 1 non-decoder model a non-experiment. A full table for Jaccard = 1.0 and ∆CFI = 0.00 is unnecessary. However, this does correctly surface the real issue: IRT misfit comes from multidimensionality, not architecture heterogeneity.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Questions for Authors (Prioritized)\n\n**Q1 [Critical].** What is the B+C DIF rate after Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at q = 0.05 across the 12,508 χ² tests? Does the headline 31.3% figure survive formal multiple-comparisons control?\n\n**Q2 [Critical].** When the MH matching variable is replaced by bifactor general-factor scores instead of sum scores, does the temporal DIF rate change substantially? This directly tests whether unidimensionality violations bias the matching step.\n\n**Q3 [Important].** The ρ ≈ 0.997 finding after removing 31% of items simultaneously validates that (a) contamination exists at scale and (b) rankings are robust. How do you reconcile these positions? What specific evaluation validity failure does temporal DIF actually cause?\n\n**Q4 [Important].** How does DIF analysis of a static benchmark complement or compete with dynamic benchmarking approaches (LiveBench, Chatbot Arena)? Under what circumstances would a practitioner choose DIF analysis over item rotation?\n\n**Q5 [Moderate].** At injection rate p = 0.1 (mild contamination), what is the ∆TPR using cross-subject matching? If detection power is negligible below p = 0.3, what does this imply for detecting real-world contamination?\n\n**Q6 [Moderate].** Among the top-50 models by original ranking, how many change rank position after DIF-item removal, and what is the maximum rank displacement?\n\n---\n\n## 6. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing paper content against the imagined experiment list from review_context.yaml:\n\n| Experiment | Plan ID | Coverage | Notes |\n|------------|---------|----------|-------|\n| MMLU DIF | plan_001 | ✅ Full (§4.1) | Core contribution, well-covered |\n| RL robustness | plan_002 | ✅ Covered (§5.1) | Appropriately caveated as underpowered |\n| ConStat eval | plan_003a | ✅ Covered (§5.2) | Jaccard=0.08, limited to 15 models |\n| DVD eval | plan_003b | ✅ Covered (§5.3) | Weak results reported honestly |\n| Ensemble fusion | plan_003c | ✅ Covered (§5.4) | Reported as negative (circularity) |\n| GSM8K cross-benchmark | plan_004 | ✅ Partial (§4.3.2, §4.4.3) | Steps 1-2 strong, Step 3 (GSM1K) underpowered |\n| Bifactor IRT | plan_005 | ✅ Covered (§5.5) | Mediocre fit, used as sensitivity analysis |\n| Item generation | plan_006 | ✅ Covered (§5.6) | Negative result, appropriately framed |\n| Cumulative Bayesian | plan_007 | ✅ Covered (§5.7) | Simulation-only, no empirical validation |\n| Graceful degradation | plan_008 | ✅ Covered (§5.8) | Clean result, validity floor at ~35% |\n| Item-Focused Trees | plan_009 | ✅ Covered (§5.9) | 78% concordance, useful robustness check |\n| Architecture stratification | plan_010 | ✅ Covered (§4.1.3) | Trivially null (99.98% decoder-only) |\n| Semi-synthetic | plan_011 | ✅ Covered (§4.3) | Core contribution, strongest evidence |\n\n**Coverage verdict:** All 13 imagined experiments are represented. No experiment is missing from the paper. The paper achieves comprehensive coverage of the experimental design, though several experiments (plan_002, plan_003a-c, plan_004 Step 3) are acknowledged as underpowered or negative.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Missing References\n\nThe paper cites only 10 references. The following are critical omissions for a cross-disciplinary paper:\n\n**Dynamic benchmarking (directly competing approaches):**\n- White et al. (2024). \"LiveBench: A Challenging, Contamination-Free LLM Benchmark.\" arXiv:2406.19314\n- Chiang et al. (2024). \"Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference.\" ICML 2024\n\n**IRT for NLP (existing bridge work):**\n- Lalor et al. (2016, 2019). IRT for NLP evaluation. EMNLP.\n- Vania et al. (2021). \"Comparing Test Sets with Item Response Theory.\" ACL 2021.\n\n**Contamination detection (incomplete coverage):**\n- Golchin & Surdeanu (2023). \"Time Travel in LLMs: Tracing Data Contamination.\" ICLR 2024.\n- Deng et al. (2024). \"Investigating Data Contamination in Modern Benchmarks.\" NAACL 2024.\n\n**Distribution shift (foundational framing):**\n- Recht et al. (2019). \"Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?\" ICML 2019.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Roundtable Convergence Summary\n\n### Areas of Full Agreement\n1. IRT misspecification (CFI = 0.72) is a major concern insufficiently resolved by the bifactor pilot\n2. Cross-subject matching is the paper's strongest and most original contribution\n3. Honest negative results reporting raises credibility of positive findings\n4. GSM1K N = 33 is underpowered and should be exploratory only\n5. Soundness = 2/4\n\n### Areas of Partial Agreement\n1. **Contribution:** R1 values empirical scale more (3/4); R2 applies stricter ICLR novelty bar (2/4). After cross-share, R1 acknowledged the novelty concern has merit but maintains cross-subject matching is substantial. Converged: 2.5/4.\n2. **Presentation:** R1 finds tables well-structured (3/4); R2 finds 9 supplementary analyses diluting (2/4). Both agree the paper tries to do too much. Converged: 2.5/4.\n\n### Remaining Disagreement\n- **Venue fit:** R1 considers this borderline ICLR with major revisions; R2 suggests NeurIPS Datasets & Benchmarks track would be a better fit. Not resolved.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Recommendation\n\n**Overall: 4/10 — Marginally below acceptance threshold (weak reject)**\n\nThe paper documents a real and interesting phenomenon at impressive scale. The cross-subject matching contribution (∆FPR = 0.000) is genuine, the construct-mismatch critique of web-overlap labels has value, and the honest reporting of negative results is commendable. However, four issues prevent acceptance in its current form:\n\n1. **Statistical validity:** The headline 31.3% figure lacks FDR correction across 12,508 simultaneous tests and is derived under severely misspecified 1D IRT (CFI = 0.72). Both are addressable but currently unresolved.\n2. **Novelty:** Applying a 60-year-old method to new data, while the cross-subject matching refinement is original, does not clear the ICLR novelty bar as currently framed.\n3. **Positioning:** With only 10 references and no engagement with dynamic benchmarking (the field's operational solution to the problem being studied), the paper does not adequately justify its niche.\n4. **Actionability:** The central tension — 31% instability yet ρ ≈ 0.997 ranking preservation — is never resolved into practical guidance for benchmark creators.\n\n### Revision Path\nA revised version that (a) reports FDR-corrected DIF rates as primary, (b) provides matching-variable sensitivity analysis under bifactor scores, (c) adds 20+ missing references engaging with dynamic benchmarking and IRT-for-NLP, (d) trims supplementary analyses to 3–4 focused checks, and (e) reconciles the instability/validity tension with concrete practitioner recommendations would be a credible resubmission at 6/10.","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260521T062008","overall_score":"4/10 — weak reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1712,"cache_read_input_tokens":88797,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1712},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17abd94c-441a-4590-97d9-9458f106f7c0","uuid":"7e37330f-3a23-42e4-a6b0-595802ddb477"}
